<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Elisa Bonaparte</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1777">1777</a>–<a href="/years/1820">1820</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Elisa Bonaparte Baciocchi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Elisa Bonaparte, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s eldest surviving sister, was made Princess of Lucca and Piombino in 1805 and ruled it with energy, reforming its administration and promoting the marble industry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massa-tuscany">Massa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrara">Carrara</a>, which was added to her lands. From 1809 she was also Grand Duchess of Tuscany. Her rule ended with Napoleon's fall in 1814.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elisa-bonaparte.jpg" alt="Elisa Bonaparte" width="320" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pietro Benvenuti · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pietro_Benvenuti_-_Ritratto_di_Elisa_Bonaparte.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
